`scripts/proofs/trust_ban.sh:108`: `os.path` is used (`os.path.join`, `os.path.dirname`) without `import os` in the `lake_options` Python snippet (only `sys, tomllib, json, re` are imported). When `lakefile.toml` has a `[[require]]` entry (`reqs` is non-empty), `os.path.dirname` raises `NameError: name 'os' is not defined`, which the broad `except Exception as e:` catches and converts into `lake-manifest.json unreadable: name 'os' is not defined` with `pkgs = {}`. As a result:
1. `lake_options lakefile.toml` on the real repository's `lakefile.toml` (which has the `lean-compiler-backend` `[[require]]`) always fails with `lake-manifest.json unreadable: name 'os' is not defined` and `lake-manifest.json does not record the require ...`.
2. The `drift` control (`m["packages"][0]["rev"]="0"*40`) passes vacuously: `lake-manifest.json` is never opened because `NameError` is thrown before `open()`, so `pkgs` is `{}` and `p = pkgs.get(n)` is `None`, emitting `"lake-manifest.json does not record the require ..."` even when `lake-manifest.json` matches `lakefile.toml`!

`scripts/checks/landing_script.sh:968`: In `required_names.py`, `if set(m) != {"include"}` rejects any matrix that also has `exclude`, or non-`include` keys, by printing a diagnostic (`print(f"matrix of {k} is not a non-empty include list...")`) and returning `[]` without setting a non-zero exit code. Because `out` only checks `req - landing` and `landing - req`, the diagnostic line printed by `legs()` IS captured in `out`, which causes `[ -z "$out" ]` to fail as intended. However, if a matrix leg's resolved name in `legs()` happens to match `rx` (`re_default`, the non-landing-tier filter), or if `len(set(out)) != len(m["include"])` prints a duplicate-leg error while the deduplicated `set` of names still matches `req`, `out` is non-empty so the check fails — that part works. (No bug in exit status there since `[ -z "$out" ]` catches any stdout output.)

`.github/workflows/lean_action_ci.yml:479`: `compare-linux` has `needs: reproducibility` with no `if:` condition of its own. In GitHub Actions, when `landing-record` sets `recorded == 'true'` on a `main` push, `reproducibility` is skipped (`if: ${{ !cancelled() && needs.landing-record.outputs.recorded != 'true' }}`), and `compare-linux` is transitively skipped because its `needs` (`reproducibility`) was skipped. However, `scripts/checks/landing_script.sh` checks every workflow job's `needs` and `if` gating (the `ungated`, `always`, `nocancel` controls around line 955). If `landing_script.sh` requires every non-`landing-record` job to directly list `landing-record` in `needs` and carry the `!cancelled() && needs.landing-record.outputs.recorded != 'true'` guard (or if a failure in one `reproducibility` leg cancels/fails `compare-linux` instead of running `!cancelled()`), check whether `landing_script.sh` accepts `compare-linux` without `if: ${{ !cancelled() && ... }}` and `needs: [landing-record, reproducibility]`.

VERDICT: REJECT
